window.docContents[119].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory":{"contents":"variable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\nnamespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nAlgebraic graph theory studies a graph via algebraic objects attached to it: its\ngroup of automorphisms, and matrices indexed by its vertices, above all the adjacency\nmatrix. In this chapter we look at both, and at how they interact.\n\nThe automorphisms of a graph G, i.e. the isomorphisms G ≃g G, form a group\nthat measures how symmetric G is. In Maps we met\nautomorphisms and vertex-transitive graphs, in which any vertex can be sent to any\nother by an automorphism, and showed that cycle graphs and hypercubes are\nvertex-transitive. In Distance Invariants Under Isomorphism we\nsaw that all the vertices of a vertex-transitive graph have the same eccentricity, so\nits radius equals its diameter.\n\nWe first note that G ≃g G is a group and that isomorphic graphs have isomorphic\nautomorphism groups, and then compute the automorphism groups of some of our running\nexamples. We start with a graph with no symmetry: the infinite path pathNat₁, whose\nautomorphism group is trivial. Next we identify the automorphism group of the cycle\ngraph C[n] as a dihedral group.\n\nWe then turn to matrices. The adjacency matrix A of a finite graph determines the\ngraph and its powers count walks. A permutation of the vertices is an automorphism\niff it preserves A, so isomorphic graphs have the same eigenvalues, and in a\nvertex-transitive graph every vertex lies on the same number of closed walks of each\nlength. We also survey Mathlib's results on the Laplacian, including the theorem\nthat the number of connected components of a graph is the dimension of the kernel\nof its Laplacian, and on strongly regular graphs such as the Petersen graph.\n\nNext we return to the hypercube: we show that two of the constructions of Q[n]\nfrom Hypercube give isomorphic graphs, and use the Hamming-graph\nmodel to prove that the automorphism group of Q[n] is the hyperoctahedral group\nℤ/2 ≀ Sₙ, of order 2ⁿ · n!.\n\nAutomorphisms also let us count copies of one graph in another: the number of\nlabelled copies is the number of unlabelled copies times the number of automorphisms.\nWe apply this to count the cycles in a graph and the 5-cycles in the Mycielskian of a\ngraph with no triangles and no 5-cycles. Finally, combining this counting identity\nwith the automorphism group of the hypercube, we show that every copy of Q[k] in\nQ[n] is an induced subcube, and count these copies.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"12. Algebraic Graph Theory","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#chapter-algebraic-graph-theory"},"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks":{"contents":"* Walk.pathNat₁Walk: walk on the infinite path from i to i + j.* Walk.pathGraphWalk: walk along the finite path graph P[n + 1] from 0 to\n  Fin.last n.* Walk.cycleGraphCycle: walk once around C[n + 3], built by transferring\n  pathGraphWalk and closing it with the wrap-around edge.* HypercubeVert.hammingPath: path between two hypercube vertices.* HypercubeVert.hammingDist: Hamming distance between two hypercube vertices\n  (number of differing coordinates).* HypercubeVert.grayStart / HypercubeVert.grayEnd: start and end vertices of the\n  Gray code walk.* Walk.grayWalk: Gray code walk on Q[n].* Walk.mycielskianAux: auxiliary zig-zag walk in mμ[G].* Walk.mycielskian: walk in mμ[G] constructed from a walk in G (protected, to\n  coexist with the graph constructor mycielskian).* Walk.mycielskianClosed: cycle in mμ[G] constructed from a cycle in G.* Walk.posaRotate: Pósa rotation of a path walk.* Walk.posaClosed: closing a Pósa path to obtain a cycle.* IsClosed: a closed graph — one with no eligible pair, i.e. any two distinct\n  vertices with degree sum at least |V| are adjacent.* modAux: fuel-limited repeated subtraction of b from a: a toy example of the\n  fuel idiom.* closureStep: one round of the closure process — join every eligible pair at once.* closureAux: fuel-limited iteration of closureStep.* closure: the Bondy–Chvátal closure of a graph (computable).* IsOrePair: Ore's condition at a pair of vertices — if distinct and non-adjacent,\n  their degree sum is at least |V|.* IsOreGraph: an Ore graph — every pair of vertices satisfies Ore's condition\n  IsOrePair.* Walk.IsMaximalPath: a maximal path — a path that cannot be extended at either\n  end, i.e. every neighbour of either endpoint already lies on it.\n\nFrom the exercises:\n\n* Walk.grayCycle: Gray code Hamiltonian cycle in Q[n + 2].* chordedC5: C[5] with the two chords 0 — 2 and 0 — 3, a graph that is not\n  an Ore graph but whose closure is complete.* Walk.IsMaximumPath: a maximum-length path — a path such that no path in G is\n  longer.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Appendix\u0009Definitions","header":"14.2.7. Chapter 8: Walks","id":"/Appendix/#section-chapter-8-walks"},"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-finiteness-without-data-the-finite-typeclass":{"contents":"Sometimes we know a type is finite but have no canonical enumeration of it, or we\nsimply don't want to drag Fintype data, and the Decidable instances it forces,\nthrough a proof. For this Mathlib provides the Finite typeclass.\n\n#source Finite -- class inductive Finite (α : Sort*) : Prop\n              --    | intro {n : ℕ} : α ≃ Fin n → Finite _\n\n\nexample {α : Type*} : Finite α ↔ ∃ n, Nonempty (α ≃ Fin n) :=\n  finite_iff_exists_equiv_fin\n\n\nThe crucial difference is that Finite V is a proposition whereas Fintype V is\ndata:\n\n* Aspect* Fintype V* Finite V\n* Nature* data (a bundled structure)* a proposition (Prop-valued)\n* Carries* a Finset V of all elements* only ∃ n, Nonempty (V ≃ Fin n)\n* Says* how to enumerate V* that V can be enumerated\n* Two instances* always equal, not always by definition* equal by definition\n* Computable?* if the instance is (Fintype.card, decide)* no (use Nat.card)\n\nWe always have Fintype V → Finite V; the reverse recovers data from a proof, so it\nneeds the axiom of choice and is noncomputable (but is still very useful in proofs).\n\n#check Finite.of_fintype   -- [Fintype α] : Finite α\n#source Fintype.ofFinite   -- noncomputable def Fintype.ofFinite\n                           --       (α : Type*) [Finite α] : Fintype α\n\n\nSo being data is not the same as being computable. Fintype.ofFinite, like any\ninstance built with the help of the classical tactic, is perfectly good Fintype\ndata for use in proofs, but #eval cannot run it.\n\nOne final remark about Fintype instances is that because two instances for the same\ntype need not be equal by definition, they can be the source of diamonds in\ntypeclass inference. This can make proofs fail in mysterious ways. We will see a\nsimple example of one below in The Handshake Lemma.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Finite Types\u0009Cardinality","header":"4.2.4.3. Finiteness Without Data: the  Finite  Typeclass","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-finiteness-without-data-the-finite-typeclass"}});